# Stay on Flutter stable

CI, Cursor Cloud, and this repo track current Flutter **stable**. Local SDK drift hides package/SDK breaks (for example `font_awesome_flutter` 10.x vs `IconData` becoming `final` in 3.44+).

When doing Flutter work, check whether the local SDK is current stable (`flutter --version` vs `flutter upgrade --dry-run`, or an equivalent stable-channel check).

If it is behind:

- Tell the user they are not on latest stable, with both versions if known.
- Suggest `flutter upgrade` (this install is `/Users/ward/workspace/flutter` on the stable channel).
- Do not run the upgrade unless they ask.
- Do not lower CI/Cloud to match an old local SDK, and do not raise `pubspec.yaml`’s `flutter: ">=3.38.1"` floor just because stable moved.
